From a8215b29adf4fb12710f05ea0102a2c6b617a077 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 05:58:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] HID: usbhid: clear halt after a transaction error on the IN
 endpoint

When the interrupt IN URB completes with -EPROTO (or -EILSEQ, -ETIME,
-ETIMEDOUT), hid_irq_in() calls hid_io_error(), which resubmits the URB
after a delay and resets the device if errors persist for a second.
Only -EPIPE is followed by usb_clear_halt().

That is not enough on xHCI. A transaction error halts the endpoint
inside the host controller. xhci-hcd can retry in place a few times
(Soft Retry), but not for a device behind the transaction translator of
an external high-speed hub: process_bulk_intr_td() skips the retry when
TT_SLOT is set in the slot context. Once the URB is to be given back
with -EPROTO, finish_td() calls xhci_handle_halted_endpoint() with
EP_HARD_RESET. That is a Reset Endpoint command without TSP, which puts
the host side data toggle back to DATA0. Nothing is sent to the device,
whose toggle stays where it was. If the next report of the device then
carries DATA1, the host takes it for a retransmission and drops it,
while the device considers it delivered. USB 2.0 5.7.5 expects a halt
caused by transmission errors to be removed through the control pipe,
which resets the toggle on both sides. usbhid only resubmits.

This was seen on v7.3.4 with a full-speed FIDO token (1050:0407) behind
two external hubs on an xHCI root port, used through hidraw by libfido2.
The idle IN URB fails with -EPROTO and is resubmitted, the next request
goes out on the OUT endpoint, and its one-packet reply never arrives.
usbmon, lines of the FIDO interface only:

ffff899842a9ccc0 3684652382 C Ii:1:058:4 -71:2 0
ffff899842a9ccc0 3684667001 S Ii:1:058:4 -115:2 64 <
ffff899842a9ccc0 3684810522 C Ii:1:058:4 -71:2 0
ffff899842a9ccc0 3684837004 S Ii:1:058:4 -115:2 64 <
ffff89a42ed66cc0 3684886020 S Io:1:058:4 -115:2 64 = ffffffff 86000840 264cf9b1 d5224d00 00000000 00000000 00000000 00000000
ffff89a42ed66cc0 3684887536 C Io:1:058:4 0:2 64 >
ffff899842a9ccc0 3685380717 C Ii:1:058:4 -71:2 0
ffff899842a9ccc0 3685433098 S Ii:1:058:4 -115:2 64 <
ffff899afe017800 3697300652 S Io:1:058:4 -115:2 64 = ffffffff 860008ff 61809f1f fe2af700 00000000 00000000 00000000 00000000
ffff899afe017800 3697301591 C Io:1:058:4 0:2 64 >
ffff899842a9ccc0 3697303578 C Ii:1:058:4 0:2 64 = ffffffff 860011ff 61809f1f fe2af78a facdbf02 05040305 00000000 00000000

The request at 3684.886 is never answered and the caller gives up after
ten seconds; the retry at 3697.300 works. In a two hour capture with
3973 requests there were 16 such error episodes on this endpoint. 13 of
them were followed by a request: its first reply was lost 7 times and
arrived 6 times. No reply was lost without an -EPROTO before it. That
fits a toggle which is wrong half of the time, but the packets on the
wire have not been looked at with a bus analyzer.

Remember the error in a new HID_IN_RESYNC flag and let hid_start_in()
defer to the reset work while it is set. The work calls
usb_clear_halt() and then submits the URB. The request puts the device
toggle at DATA0 and usb_reset_endpoint() does the same for the host,
which xhci-hcd skips when its hard reset has just done it. The retry
delays of hid_io_error() and its escalation to a device reset stay as
they are. When the request fails, the URB is submitted anyway, as it
was before. The flag survives close and suspend, so the endpoint is
brought back in sync before whatever submission comes next, and a
device reset clears it.

This is not specific to transaction translators: every transaction
error that xhci-hcd passes on as -EPROTO for an interrupt endpoint has
been through a hard reset. With a TT there are just no retries before
it. Other host controller drivers gain one control request per error.

As noted in the first thread below, resetting both toggles can in
principle let a packet whose acknowledgment was lost earlier be accepted
a second time. The alternative is the silent loss described above.

The mismatch and who should repair it were discussed on linux-usb in
March and April 2026. Doing it in xhci-hcd was not wanted, doing it in
usbcore was started but is unfinished, and class drivers calling
usb_clear_halt() themselves were considered fine. Michal Pecio
reproduced the loss with a keyboard and saw it go away when -EPROTO was
handled like -EPIPE (bugzilla 221184, comment 38). Unlike that
experiment this patch keeps the retry delay and does not reset the
device when the request fails, which it easily does right after an
error.

The analysis and the patch were prepared with an LLM coding assistant.

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-usb/20260313085354.71a6dbf1.michal.pecio@gmail.com/
Link: https://bugzilla.kernel.org/show_bug.cgi?id=221184
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-usb/2604e951-01e8-44d0-a11e-be63b0849c23@linux.intel.com/
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-usb/20260928120343.49e02c07.michal.pecio@gmail.com/
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-usb/20250918112128.05c21d6b.michal.pecio@gmail.com/
---
 drivers/hid/usbhid/hid-core.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/hid/usbhid/usbhid.h   |  5 +++++
 2 files changed, 30 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/hid/usbhid/hid-core.c b/drivers/hid/usbhid/hid-core.c
index d7cf2b56e117..229bf5e65cf7 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/usbhid/hid-core.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/usbhid/hid-core.c
@@ -91,6 +91,12 @@ static int hid_start_in(struct hid_device *hid)
 	    !test_bit(HID_DISCONNECTED, &usbhid->iofl) &&
 	    !test_bit(HID_SUSPENDED, &usbhid->iofl) &&
 	    !test_and_set_bit(HID_IN_RUNNING, &usbhid->iofl)) {
+		if (test_bit(HID_IN_RESYNC, &usbhid->iofl)) {
+			/* hid_reset() clears the halt, then comes back here */
+			clear_bit(HID_IN_RUNNING, &usbhid->iofl);
+			schedule_work(&usbhid->reset_work);
+			goto unlock;
+		}
 		rc = usb_submit_urb(usbhid->urbin, GFP_ATOMIC);
 		if (rc != 0) {
 			clear_bit(HID_IN_RUNNING, &usbhid->iofl);
@@ -100,6 +106,7 @@ static int hid_start_in(struct hid_device *hid)
 			clear_bit(HID_NO_BANDWIDTH, &usbhid->iofl);
 		}
 	}
+unlock:
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&usbhid->lock, flags);
 	return rc;
 }
@@ -136,6 +143,22 @@ static void hid_reset(struct work_struct *work)
 		}
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A transaction error can leave the host (xHCI resets its side) and
+	 * the device with different data toggles: resync before polling again.
+	 */
+	if (test_bit(HID_IN_RESYNC, &usbhid->iofl) &&
+	    !test_bit(HID_IN_RUNNING, &usbhid->iofl) &&
+	    !test_bit(HID_RESET_PENDING, &usbhid->iofl)) {
+		rc = usb_clear_halt(hid_to_usb_dev(hid), usbhid->urbin->pipe);
+		if (rc)
+			dev_dbg(&usbhid->intf->dev,
+				"clear-halt after I/O error failed: %d\n", rc);
+		clear_bit(HID_IN_RESYNC, &usbhid->iofl);
+		if (hid_start_in(hid))
+			hid_io_error(hid);
+	}
+
 	if (test_bit(HID_RESET_PENDING, &usbhid->iofl)) {
 		dev_dbg(&usbhid->intf->dev, "resetting device\n");
 		usb_queue_reset_device(usbhid->intf);
@@ -314,6 +337,7 @@ static void hid_irq_in(struct urb *urb)
 	case -ETIME:		/* protocol error or unplug */
 	case -ETIMEDOUT:	/* Should never happen, but... */
 		usbhid_mark_busy(usbhid);
+		set_bit(HID_IN_RESYNC, &usbhid->iofl);
 		clear_bit(HID_IN_RUNNING, &usbhid->iofl);
 		hid_io_error(hid);
 		return;
@@ -1584,6 +1608,7 @@ static int hid_post_reset(struct usb_interface *intf)
 	spin_lock_irq(&usbhid->lock);
 	clear_bit(HID_RESET_PENDING, &usbhid->iofl);
 	clear_bit(HID_CLEAR_HALT, &usbhid->iofl);
+	clear_bit(HID_IN_RESYNC, &usbhid->iofl);
 	spin_unlock_irq(&usbhid->lock);
 	hid_set_idle(dev, intf->cur_altsetting->desc.bInterfaceNumber, 0, 0);
 
diff --git a/drivers/hid/usbhid/usbhid.h b/drivers/hid/usbhid/usbhid.h
index 75fe85d3d27a..0287eb22e3bf 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/usbhid/usbhid.h
+++ b/drivers/hid/usbhid/usbhid.h
@@ -47,6 +47,11 @@ struct usb_interface *usbhid_find_interface(int minor);
  * device.
  */
 #define HID_IN_POLLING		14
+/*
+ * The IN URB got a transaction error: clear the halt before submitting it
+ * again, the host controller may have reset its data toggle.
+ */
+#define HID_IN_RESYNC		15
 
 /*
  * USB-specific HID struct, to be pointed to

